Writeup: TryHackMe Nmap Room

Nmap

Link : https://tryhackme.com/room/furthernmap
Platform: TryHackMe (free room)


1. Overview

This room gives an in-depth look at port scanning with Nmap. It explains why scanning is the first step of enumeration, the main switches, each scan type (connect, SYN, UDP, NULL, FIN, Xmas, ping sweep), the Nmap Scripting Engine (NSE) and firewall evasion, and ends with a practical scan of a lab machine.

2. Learning objectives

Explain what a port is, how many exist and which are "well known".
Use the common Nmap switches for scan type, verbosity, output, speed and port selection.
Describe how each scan type works on the wire and what response means open, closed or filtered.
Find and run NSE scripts and read their help.
Scan hosts that block ping, and understand firewall evasion options.

3. Task-by-task notes

Task 1 and 2: Deploy and Introduction

Deployed the target machine. It is for scanning only, so no login or exploitation is needed.
A computer has 65,535 TCP ports (and the same range for UDP). Ports direct traffic to the right application, and a client uses a random high port to connect to a server's service port such as 443.
Ports 1 to 1023 are the well-known (standard) ports. Port scanning is the first step of enumeration because you cannot attack what you do not know is running.
Nmap reports a port as open, closed or filtered.
